{"id": "galapagos", "name": "Galápagos Islands", "short": "Galápagos", "place": "Ecuador (Pacific Ocean)", "lat": -0.7, "lon": -90.5, "side": "l", "cont": "South America", "country": "Ecuador", "blurb": "Cold upwelling feeds sea lions, penguins and seaweed-eating iguanas.", "story": "The Galápagos are volcanic islands on the equator, about 900 km west of South America, famous for their many endemic species. Cold, nutrient-rich water feeds the sardines and plankton that feed sea lions and penguins, and marine iguanas are the only lizards that forage in the sea, grazing on algae.", "matters": "Studied by Charles Darwin in the 1830s, the islands' wildlife helped inspire his theory of evolution by natural selection. All of the islands are protected as part of Ecuador's Galápagos National Park and Marine Reserve.", "tip": {"id": "upwelling", "title": "Switch off the upwelling", "text": "In El Niño years the cold, nutrient-rich current stops rising near the islands. Cut Cold upwelling and watch algae, sardines, iguanas, penguins and sea lions."}, "sources": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Galápagos Islands", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal%C3%A1pagos_Islands"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Marine iguana", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marine_iguana"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Galápagos tortoise", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal%C3%A1pagos_tortoise"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Flightless cormorant", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flightless_cormorant"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Galápagos sea lion", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gal%C3%A1pagos_sea_lion"}, {"title": "Wikipedia: Cromwell Current", "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cromwell_Current"}], "notes": "Feeding links were checked against the sources listed where they state them. Where the sources are silent, links come from general ecological knowledge and are illustrative. Feeding links that match a GloBI (Global Biotic Interactions) record are marked in the node details, with a link to the records. A few links were added from GloBI records.", "imgwiki": "Galápagos Islands", "icon": "turtle", "tier": "overview", "nodes": [{"id": "phyto", "sp": "drifting algae", "xh": 0.1}, {"id": "algae", "sp": "red and green seaweeds", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "opuntia", "sp": "opuntia species", "xh": 0.66}, {"id": "grass", "sp": "island grasses", "xh": 0.86}, {"id": "zoop", "sp": "copepods and larvae", "xh": 0.06, "desc": "Tiny drifting grazers that eat phytoplankton and are in turn eaten by small schooling fish."}, {"id": "sardine", "sp": "sardines and similar", "xh": 0.17}, {"id": "crab", "sp": "grapsus grapsus", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "iguana", "sp": "amblyrhynchus cristatus", "xh": 0.42}, {"id": "finch", "sp": "geospiza and relatives", "xh": 0.56}, {"id": "tortoise", "sp": "chelonoidis species", "xh": 0.7}, {"id": "goat", "sp": "capra hircus", "xh": 0.86, "name": "Feral goat (introduced)"}, {"id": "cormorant", "sp": "phalacrocorax harrisi", "xh": 0.15}, {"id": "penguin", "sp": "spheniscus mendiculus", "xh": 0.27}, {"id": "sealion", "sp": "zalophus wollebaeki", "xh": 0.4}, {"id": "rat", "sp": "rattus rattus", "xh": 0.8, "name": "Black rat (introduced)", "tl": 3, "desc": "Introduced omnivore that eats eggs and hatchlings of tortoises and iguanas and has wiped out young tortoises on some islands."}, {"id": "hammerhead", "sp": "sphyrna lewini", "xh": 0.12}, {"id": "galshark", "sp": "carcharhinus galapagensis", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "hawk", "sp": "buteo galapagoensis", "xh": 0.74}, {"id": "sun", "sp": "sunlight", "xh": 0.1, "desc": "Equatorial sunlight powers algae at sea and desert plants on land."}, {"id": "upwelling", "sp": "cromwell current and humboldt current", "xh": 0.3}, {"id": "rain", "sp": "seasonal rain and mist", "xh": 0.68}, {"id": "decomp", "sp": "bacteria and fungi", "xh": 0.92, "desc": "Break down dead plants and animals, returning nutrients to soil and sea."}], "raw": [["sun", "phyto", "r"], ["sun", "algae", "r"], ["sun", "opuntia", "r"], ["sun", "grass", "r"], ["upwel